<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11737"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 17, 2005 - 2:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is no Chinese aphabet since it is comprised of thousands of characters ususally using two characters together to create a word but on this link are some of the more common characters used in the printing of the Chinese language. <BR>Look at the 12th character from the left just above the number 1 in both rows of simplified and traditional chinese, it closely resembles the BTK symbol. <BR><a href="http://www.ask-group.co.uk/publish/chinesealph.htm" target=_top>http://www.ask-group.co.uk/publish/chinesealph.htm</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
